    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Here's what I changed and why.\n\n## Summary\n\nRedesigned owner identity so `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, with all values built from it following automatically.\n\n**`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 rewrote `service` to build the code as `region + \"-\" + hash8`:\n- `REGION` comes from the postcode (city as fallback) via `OwnerLocality.forPostcode`.\n- `HASH8` is the first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName` (the telephone is already E.164-normalized by `BuildOwner` at this point).\n- Dropped the per-city sequence number entirely, so the step no longer needs `OwnerRepository`.\n\n**`OwnerLocality.java`** \u2014 split the two concerns:\n- `forPostcode(postcode, city)` holds the existing postcode\u2192region logic (now the source of the code's `REGION` half).\n- `region(Owner)` now reads the region back off the `customerCode` prefix, so the derived `locality` moves onto the new identity.\n\n**Untouched by design** \u2014 the membership number and check digit (`OwnerMapper`, `CustomerCodeCheckDigit`) and the create audit record (`AuditOwnerCreated`) all already read `customerCode`, so they moved to the new identity for free. The audit line confirms it: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\n## Structural impact\nTwo files, two changed functions (`AssignCustomerCode.service`, `OwnerLocality.region`), each small and in low-complexity classes. Keeping the locality change inside `OwnerLocality` (whose only caller, `OwnerMapper`, keeps its existing `OwnerLocality.region(owner)` call site) avoided editing the large MapStruct mapper class, keeping `WMC_other` low. All tests pass (`AcceptanceTest` asserts the code matches `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`).",
